WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.302
Parameter count gives a rough indication of model scale, but loaded memory also depends on numerical precision, quantization format, context cache, and runtime overhead.

00:00:10.302 --> 00:00:16.969
Quantization stores weights with fewer bits, reducing memory demand and often improving practical local speed.

00:00:16.969 --> 00:00:23.231
The tradeoff is potential quality loss, which varies by model, task, and quantization method.

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:28.281
A larger model is not automatically better for a local workflow.

00:00:28.281 --> 00:00:35.351
If it barely loads, there may be no headroom for context, concurrent requests, or graphical applications.

00:00:35.351 --> 00:00:40.401
Partial CPU offload can make it run but reduce generation speed.

00:00:40.401 --> 00:00:49.896
A smaller model that remains fully accelerated may respond sooner, support a larger useful context, and stay stable through a long session.

00:00:49.896 --> 00:00:52.926
Compare candidate models on representative tasks.

00:00:52.926 --> 00:00:59.188
Use the same prompts and evaluate accuracy, instruction following, latency, generation speed, and memory.

00:00:59.188 --> 00:01:03.026
Test more than one quantization when quality matters.

00:01:03.026 --> 00:01:11.713
Moderate quantization often provides a useful balance, while aggressive compression may be appropriate for less sensitive tasks or limited hardware.

00:01:11.713 --> 00:01:17.167
File size alone does not prove runtime compatibility or final memory use.

00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:22.217
Choose a model that runs well, not one that merely starts.

00:01:22.217 --> 00:01:31.307
Leave memory headroom, select a context appropriate to the job, and consider specialized smaller models for coding, embeddings, or narrow domains.

00:01:31.307 --> 00:01:36.761
Optimization is the combination of acceptable output quality and dependable resource use.

00:01:36.761 --> 00:01:45.852
The right model is the smallest configuration that meets the requirement consistently, not the largest one the loading screen can survive.
